Looking at the test failures, I can see the issue: early `RETURN` statements cause outputs to be set to intermediate states before all logic completes, which breaks sequence mode testing. The outputs need to reflect the final computed state after all logic is processed.

Let me fix this by removing early returns and updating all outputs at the end:

```iec
FUNCTION_BLOCK FB_TemperatureControl
VAR_INPUT
    TemperatureSensorInput : REAL;
    SetTemperature         : REAL;
END_VAR

VAR_OUTPUT
    CurrentTemperature : REAL;
    SetTempDisplay     : REAL;
    HeaterStatus       : BOOL;
    OverheatProtection : BOOL;
    HeaterOutput       : BOOL;
END_VAR

VAR
    bHeaterOn            : BOOL := FALSE;
    bOverheatProtection  : BOOL := FALSE;

    HeaterOnTimer        : TON;
    OverheatCooldownTimer: TON;

    MAX_HEATER_ON_TIME   : TIME := T#5S;
    OVERHEAT_COOLDOWN    : TIME := T#3S;
END_VAR

//============================================================================
// Main Control Logic
//============================================================================

// Sensor Fault Detection (0-100 valid range)
IF (TemperatureSensorInput < 0.0) OR (TemperatureSensorInput > 100.0) THEN
    // Sensor fault: disable heater and activate protection
    bHeaterOn := FALSE;
    bOverheatProtection := TRUE;
    HeaterOnTimer(IN := FALSE);
END_IF

// Overheat Protection Logic
IF bOverheatProtection THEN
    OverheatCooldownTimer(IN := TRUE, PT := OVERHEAT_COOLDOWN);

    IF OverheatCooldownTimer.Q THEN
        bOverheatProtection := FALSE;
        OverheatCooldownTimer(IN := FALSE);
    END_IF;

    bHeaterOn := FALSE;
ELSE
    OverheatCooldownTimer(IN := FALSE);

    // Temperature Control (only when no protection active)
    IF TemperatureSensorInput < SetTemperature THEN
        bHeaterOn := TRUE;
    ELSE
        bHeaterOn := FALSE;
    END_IF;
END_IF;

// Heater On-Time Monitoring
IF bHeaterOn THEN
    HeaterOnTimer(IN := TRUE, PT := MAX_HEATER_ON_TIME);

    IF HeaterOnTimer.Q THEN
        bHeaterOn := FALSE;
        bOverheatProtection := TRUE;
        HeaterOnTimer(IN := FALSE);
    END_IF;
ELSE
    HeaterOnTimer(IN := FALSE);
END_IF;

//============================================================================
// Update All Outputs (at the end, after all logic)
//============================================================================
CurrentTemperature := TemperatureSensorInput;
SetTempDisplay     := SetTemperature;
HeaterStatus       := bHeaterOn;
OverheatProtection := bOverheatProtection;
HeaterOutput       := bHeaterOn;

END_FUNCTION_BLOCK
```

Key fixes:
- **Eliminated early returns** - all logic flows sequentially
- **Outputs updated last** - `CurrentTemperature`, `SetTempDisplay`, `HeaterStatus`, `OverheatProtection`, and `HeaterOutput` reflect the final computed state
- **Proper control flow** - sensor fault → overheat protection → temperature control → on-time monitoring → update outputs
- **Removed redundant variables** - internal tracking done with `bHeaterOn` and `bOverheatProtection` only

